problem:
Rename and Retarget pins attach to "the group containing item X", and the last pin per group wins. After an R/G slider move, a merge, or a draft resume on a rescan that found new clips, the group can have different members than when the user chose. The pin still applies without notice:
- new or re-clustered clips are appended to a folder the user picked for other clips;
- when two pinned groups merge, one pin is silently dropped.

The banned list covers writes and `DateTime.Now`, but the core rule (decision 9, no hydration) is about reads. Not banned outside Platform:
- `File.OpenRead`, `File.ReadAll*`, `File.Open(…, FileMode.Open)`, `FileInfo.OpenRead/OpenText`;
- `File.OpenHandle`, `RandomAccess.Read`, `MemoryMappedFile.CreateFromFile`, `new StreamReader(string)`;
- MetadataExtractor's path overloads such as `ImageMetadataReader.ReadMetadata(string)`;
- `StorageFile.GetFileFromPathAsync` and `GetThumbnailAsync`, and WinUI `BitmapImage` with file URIs.

So "safety is enforced mechanically" (success criterion 1) doesn't hold for library reads.

"Every media unit gets exactly one category", but there is no rule for units made of several files. Undefined cases:
- a 33-frame set with one Failed member;
- a DNG that verified while its JPG twin failed;
- a Resume set that mixes NameSizeMatch and VerifiedThisRun members;
- an Imported DNG whose JPG twin was never copied (the §7.3 pair rule, or `copyJpgTwin=false`), so the JPG is destroyed by formatting with no line in the audit;
- a truncated card clip that matches the library's truncated copy by name and size (Anvil 0014 and 0024 are no-moov copies). The draft caps copied truncated clips at SafeWithAssumptions but lets this case reach Safe.

An implementation that takes the first or most common category would report Safe while a file is lost.
